  <h1>India’s Healthcare Infrastructure Post-COVID — Progress and Gaps</h1>
  <div><em>Indian Express | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>The COVID-19 pandemic exposed the fragility of India’s healthcare system but also triggered reforms in public health infrastructure. By 2024, the government had expanded facilities, digital health programs, and insurance coverage. However, gaps in funding, manpower, and rural access persisted. The Indian Express highlighted how India’s healthcare sector remains at a crossroads — making strides post-COVID but still struggling with structural weaknesses.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Achievements Since COVID-19<br/>1.	Digital Health Mission<br/>o	Ayushman Bharat Digital Mission (ABDM): Creation of digital health IDs, telemedicine expansion, and e-hospitals.<br/>o	Widespread use of teleconsultation under e-Sanjeevani, which crossed 150 million consultations by 2024.<br/>2.	Ayushman Bharat Scheme<br/>o	Health coverage of up to ₹5 lakh for 50 crore people.<br/>o	Expansion of Health & Wellness Centres (HWCs) — >1.6 lakh operational.<br/>3.	Vaccine Infrastructure<br/>o	India became a leading global vaccine supplier post-COVID, strengthening its pharma sector.<br/>4.	Emergency Preparedness<br/>o	New biosecurity labs, oxygen plants, and stockpiling of critical drugs.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Persistent Gaps<br/>•	Low Public Spending: ~2.1% of GDP (2024) on health vs WHO’s recommended 5%.<br/>•	Manpower Shortage: Doctor-patient ratio ~1:1,500 (below WHO norm of 1:1,000).<br/>•	Rural–Urban Divide: 65% of population lives in rural areas but majority of doctors concentrated in cities.<br/>•	Out-of-Pocket Expenditure (OOPE): Still ~48% of total health spending, pushing families into poverty.<br/>•	Infrastructure Deficits: Lack of ICU beds, diagnostics, and secondary care in districts.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Policy Initiatives<br/>•	National Health Policy 2017: Target of increasing spending to 2.5% GDP by 2025.<br/>•	PM Ayushman Bharat Health Infrastructure Mission (PM-ABHIM, 2021): Focus on emergency preparedness.<br/>•	Public–Private Partnerships (PPP): Encouraged for diagnostics, telemedicine, and hospital networks.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Challenges Ahead<br/>•	Governance Issues: Poor coordination between Centre and states.<br/>•	Insurance Limitations: Ayushman Bharat doesn’t cover outpatient care, where majority of OOPE occurs.<br/>•	Non-Communicable Diseases (NCDs): Rising burden of diabetes, hypertension, cancer.<br/>•	Epidemic Preparedness: Gaps remain in rural surveillance and testing.<br/>________________________________________<br/>UPSC Relevance<br/>•	GS-II (Governance): Health policies, rights-based welfare (Ayushman Bharat).<br/>•	GS-III (Economy): Health infrastructure as part of human capital.<br/>•	Essay:<br/>o	“Health is the true wealth of a nation.”<br/>o	“From pandemic preparedness to universal healthcare — India’s unfinished journey.”<br/>•	GS-IV (Ethics): Ethical responsibility of state in providing healthcare.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Raise Public Health Spending: Move toward 3% of GDP with more state-level contributions.<br/>2.	Strengthen Primary Healthcare: Upgrade HWCs into comprehensive care centers.<br/>3.	Medical Education Reforms: Expand medical colleges, nursing institutes, and paramedic training.<br/>4.	Universal Health Coverage (UHC): Cover outpatient costs, preventive care, and mental health.<br/>5.	Technology Integration: Expand telehealth, AI diagnostics, and health data governance.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>India’s healthcare sector has advanced since COVID-19 but remains underfunded and unequal. The real challenge lies in bridging rural–urban divides, expanding preventive care, and investing in manpower. For UPSC aspirants, healthcare illustrates how governance, economy, and ethics converge in a welfare state.</p>
